<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moorea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moorea is a volcanic island in the Society Islands of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, about 17 kilometres north-west of Tahiti. In the late 18th and early 19th centuries it was closely bound up with Tahitian politics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomare-ii">Pōmare II</a> took refuge there before regaining Tahiti in 1815. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> set up its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in the islands there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moorea.jpg" alt="Moorea" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DANIEL JULIE from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DSC00042_Polyn%C3%A9sia_Moor%C3%A9a_Island_Motu_Mo%C3%A9a_Lagoon_and_transportation_Boat_(8076082190).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Moʼorea lies 17 kilometres northwest of Tahiti and is about 16 kilometres across. Tahiti and Moʼorea are the largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> in the Society Islands. The name means 'yellow lizard' in Tahitian, and an older name is ʼAimeho. It formed as a shield volcano 1.5 to 2.5 million years ago from the hotspot responsible for all the Society Islands, and a coral reef now encircles it, forming a lagoon. Two nearly symmetrical bays cut the north shore, Cook's Bay, also called Pao Pao Bay, and Ōpūnohu Bay. Charles Darwin, looking at the island from a peak on Tahiti, found in the encircling barrier reef inspiration for his theory of how coral atolls form, calling it a 'picture in a frame'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Society Islands were probably settled from Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> around 200 CE. On Moʼorea nine tribal principalities emerged in the enclosed valleys, each divided into clans, with an elite that combined political and religious power. Its leading families stayed linked by marriage and kinship with those of Tahiti for centuries, which brought alliances and at times bloody conflicts. Research in the Ōpūnohu Valley, begun by Kenneth P. Emory in the 1920s and continued by Roger C. Green in the 1960s, traces the change: little stratification before 1000 CE, terraces and a hierarchical society in the Atiroʼo period (1000–1650), then the Marama period (1650–1788), when the Marama chiefs united the valley's clans and built large step-pyramid marae.</p><p>Pedro Fernandes de Queirós sighted the island in 1606, and Domingo de Bonechea named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in 1774. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> arrived in October 1777, and the first contact with Europeans there is said to have begun when one of his goats was stolen. After negotiations failed, two armed parties went ashore to burn houses and boats, and the goat was returned. Cook first landed in Ōpūnohu Bay, and Cook's Bay was later named after him. Near Afareaitu, the Marae Umarea, built around 900 CE, is the oldest worship platform.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island forms the commune of Moʼorea-Maiʼao, with ʼĀfareaitu as its main village, and had 17,718 inhabitants in 2017. Most of the population is Christian. Tourism has been the main income since the 1960s, after an American company built the first luxury hotel in 1961. The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley, runs the Richard B. Gump research station on Cook's Bay, and a French research centre works at Ōpūnohu Bay on coral reefs. Tahiti is reached by ferry to Vaiʼare or by a flight of about five minutes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moʼorea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family’s influence reached <b>Moorea</b>, Tetiaroa and Mehetia, although rival chiefs long contested its claim to rule all Tahiti.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
